          ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\tan x.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/scratch/17592186176049/tangent.png)\n\nLet's examine each statement.\n\n- Statement I is true. The function $y=\\tan x$ has period $\\pi.$ So, $\\tan(x+\\pi)=\\tan(x)=\\tan(x+2n\\pi)$ for any integer $n.$\n\n- Statement II is true. The function $y=\\tan x$ has period $\\pi.$ The two arguments differ by $2 \\pi,$ which is an integer multiple of $\\pi.$ Therefore, the equality holds wherever both sides are defined. So, $\\tan(x- \\frac{\\pi}{4})=\\tan(x- \\frac{\\pi}{4}+2 \\pi)=\\tan(x+\\frac{7 \\pi}{4}).$\n\n- Statement III is false. The function is defined everywhere except where $x=\\frac{\\pi}{2}+n\\pi.$"
